Here are several JavaScript solutions to remove "(age X)" from your string, where X can be any number:

## Solution 1: Using Regular Expression with replace()

```javascript
// Original string
let text = "july 7 1994 (age 7) London, UK (1994-07-07)";

// Remove (age X) where X is any number
let cleanedText = text.replace(/\(age \d+\)\s*/g, '');

console.log(cleanedText);
// Output: "july 7 1994 London, UK (1994-07-07)"
```

## Solution 2: Function to clean any string

```javascript
function removeAge(str) {
    // This regex matches "(age " followed by one or more digits, followed by ")"
    // The \s* at the end also removes any trailing spaces
    return str.replace(/\(age \d+\)\s*/g, '');
}

// Test cases
console.log(removeAge("july 7 1994 (age 7) London, UK (1994-07-07)"));
// Output: "july 7 1994 London, UK (1994-07-07)"

console.log(removeAge("january 15 1985 (age 39) Paris, France"));
// Output: "january 15 1985 Paris, France"

console.log(removeAge("march 22 2000 (age 24) New York, USA"));
// Output: "march 22 2000 New York, USA"
```

## Solution 3: More flexible version (handles multiple digits)

```javascript
function removeAgePattern(text) {
    // \d+ matches one or more digits (handles ages like 7, 24, 100, etc.)
    // The 'g' flag ensures all occurrences are replaced (if there are multiple)
    return text.replace(/\(age \d+\)\s*/gi, '');
}

// Examples with different age values
const examples = [
    "july 7 1994 (age 7) London, UK (1994-07-07)",
    "december 31 1950 (age 73) Tokyo, Japan",
    "may 1 2010 (age 14) Berlin, Germany",
    "october 10 1920 (age 103) Rome, Italy"
];

examples.forEach(example => {
    console.log("Original:", example);
    console.log("Cleaned:", removeAgePattern(example));
    console.log("---");
});
```

## Explanation of the Regular Expression:

- `\(` - Matches the literal opening parenthesis "("
- `age ` - Matches the literal text "age " (including the space)
- `\d+` - Matches one or more digits (0-9)
- `\)` - Matches the literal closing parenthesis ")"
- `\s*` - Matches zero or more whitespace characters after the closing parenthesis
- `g` - Global flag to replace all occurrences (if there are multiple)
- `i` - (optional) Case-insensitive flag if you want to match "Age" or "AGE" as well

Choose the solution that best fits your needs. Solution 1 is the simplest for one-time use, while Solution 2 or 3 would be better if you need to reuse this functionality multiple times in your code.

